Z||000000||mblackwell||07-21-2001||05:51 PM||||My A/C doesn't work.  The previous owner said the only thing wrong with it is that it needs recharged, and he didn't know if there were any leaks.<P>It's getting really hot out, how much would it cost to get it recharged, what if there <I>is</I> a leak?  If I get anything done to it do they need to convert to the new coolant type?  Will that cost extra?||||reg||5
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||07-21-2001||06:35 PM||||well I know as of yesterday freon at auto zone if you have a licsense to buy it was $36.00for a 12oz.can of R-12 and if you take it some where to have this done I'm guessing with out knowing any thing that is or isnt wrong with you A/C they are going to charge you about $50.00 plus freon And I'm sure that they will find something very wrong as they always do And probobly charge you .dearly to get this A/C fixed .I'guess $350 to $400.00 hope I am wrong I guess what I'm saying is find a place that you can trust. good luck||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||07-21-2001||07:54 PM||||I think it cost me close to $800 to have the R12 system rebuilt a few years ago.  Cost will depend on whats wrong with it.  May be cheaper to convert to the 134a if it needs repair.  I had that done to my 88 when the compressor went.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||07-21-2001||09:13 PM||||If it were me, I'd do the 134A with a can of stop leak-just in case. &lt;$50.00 do it yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick86GT||07-21-2001||09:21 PM||||Let's assume the previous owner is right.  You can convert your system yourself to r-134a for about $30.00.  (I've done more than 10, not just Fieros, in the past 3 years...and all are still working.)<P>First, go to Advance Auto, Auto Zone or the like and buy a conversion kit.  Follow the instructions, except in Fiero, install the 8.5 oz oil/seal conditioner first followed by 2 cans R-134a (upside down), top off with a right-side up can of R-134a (if the system will accept any more)until it feels like it's blowing cold enuff...then remove the tap, quit.  The leak sealer expands the "o" rings over a short time period.  You may have to "top-off" again in a few days or weeks, but no matter, summer is short. <P>I know this is non-tech, "backyard mechanic," yet, it works. Go for it!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Loki||07-21-2001||10:02 PM||||My 2nd job at Pep-Boys it will run you about 680 or so for pretty much new everything and to switch it over to 134...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998||||reg||1
