Z||000008||stimpy||07-21-2001||10:52 PM||||I just had mine redone. (Locked up compressor) I replaced the compressor ($196 at O'Reilly's) and evaporator ($35 @ Oklahoma Air) and Precision Tune on South Western Ave in Oklahoma City installed my parts and only charged me $225 in labor & coolant. So by looking around and getting the good deal and co-operative, Fiero friendly shop, I was able to get it done for less then $500. <BR>MAJOR PROPS TO THE GUYS AT PRECISION TUNE!!!<P>(edited because of f*d up wireless WebTV keyboard.)<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||cadaver||07-22-2001||06:38 PM||||If it is empty then there IS a leak. I fixed my leak and charged it with R-12 for around$70 total.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Voytek2||07-22-2001||10:12 PM||||There is another refrigerant available.  It is fairly new on the market.  It's called Duracool.  This stuff is hydrocarbon based (I believe it's Propane) and there is NO need to convert your whole system the way it needs to be done for R134.  I believe you only need to change 2 fittings on the accumulator.  I've been doing some reading on this, since I'm in the process of repairing my A/C system.  Apparently this stuff cools more than R12 with much lower working pressure than R134.  I've talked to a local distributor, parts store and authorized shop.  Apparently, numerous conversions have been done successfully.  You can also buy this stuff in do-it-yourself cans, although I would recommend finding a shop to do the initial charge (and all else that goes with it, such as vacuuming the system, adding oil and dye to the refrigerant and the 2 new fittings).  It costs about $180.00 CDN to convert (TOTAL WITH RECHARGE).  Just something to think about.  That's what I'm going with.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Voytek2||07-22-2001||10:14 PM||||One other comment on this.  Check your local laws - apparently Duracool is illegal in 18 states (lack of research?) but very legal in Canada and Australia.||||reg||1
Z||000012||peterh||07-23-2001||11:50 AM||||Find a radiator/airconditioning speciality shop. They did my Volvo conversion for $200, used old compressor, etc. Works great, just as cool as old style freon.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||BOILERMAKER||07-23-2001||01:24 PM||||I would always recommend doing the conversion on our little friends.  It is cheap, quick, and lasts if you do it right!!  Last one I did took less than an hour (of course I had all the right parts) and is still running (makes one year now).  Oldest conversion I have is a little over 2 years old now and hasn't been recharged yet.<P>Good Stuff!!||||reg||1
